Peter Joseph Oosterhouse was a very  interesting major-general a civil war he  was one of very few German major  generals in the Civil War there were  only two others that had been immigrants  to the United States as adults I am the  great great granddaughter of Peter  Joseph foster house and I knew very  little about him when I started digging  into genealogy I looked for information  about it and I found a lot of  discrepancies in the little bit of  information that I could find and I  thought as I dug into it more and more  deeply that there's a story there  because he did a lot more than anyone  has given him credit for he he started  the war in Missouri in the very first  battle west of the Mississippi and he  ended the war signing the last surrender  document west of the Mississippi in  between he was on a fourteen hundred  mile front and he participated in almost  every major battle in the Western  theater except for Shiloh he was in  Vicksburg campaign the Chattanooga  campaign and the Atlanta Campaign the  Germans fought mostly for the north in  the Civil War because that's where they  mostly landed when they immigrated  that's where the jobs were a lot of them  were farmers but they were also skilled  or unskilled workers and there was not  that much for them to do in the south so  demographically there were way more  Germans in the north and ever were in  the south Peter Joseph who was as a  rebel in the German Revolution of 1848  and  and as his other fellow forty-eighters  who were also rebels in that war found  when they came to the United States the  the idea of the United States  disintegrating was horrible to them  because they had been fighting for unity  for a united country in Germany and now  they were seeing what they thought was a  fine united country here trying to break  up again and that just horrified them  he's been sort of forgotten I call him  the unknown general because there are  several reasons first of all the Western  campaign itself was not well covered by  the press at that time second of all he  was an ethnic and everybody just assumed  that he was commanding other ethnics and  there was limited interest in those  except as how they failed in their in  their missions I think they think that  this book uh peels to not only people  who were curious about the Western  campaign in the Western theater because  that area of scholarship has been  neglected until the last few years but  also he turns out to have been the best  German born field general of any of them  and has gotten very little attention so  I'm thinking that any German American  might be very interested in finding out  what his contribution as representative  of the two hundred and two hundred  thousand other Germans who fought in the  Civil War for the north represented it  was a much bigger contribution than he's  been given credit for
 

